<article class="article wrap-narrow">
  <header class="article-head"><h1>About Mamarr</h1>
  <p class="dek">Mamarr (Arabic for "passage" or "corridor") is an independent publication on logistics and supply chains in the Gulf and Red Sea — for the people who ship through these corridors, not for those reading about them from afar.</p></header>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why Mamarr</h2>
    <p>Global shipping news is written from London, Singapore and Rotterdam, and the Gulf appears in it when a strait closes or insurance premiums spike. What that actually means for an importer in Sohar, a forwarder in Jeddah or a procurement manager in Dammam is rarely written by anyone — and in Arabic, almost never.</p>
    <p>Mamarr fills that gap with three things: a weekly analysis explaining what each shift in corridors and ports does to an actual shipment, a rates table for Gulf lanes collected from the market rather than from global indices alone, and practical import and clearance guides written from inside the process.</p>
    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>Mamarr is edited by <strong>Mohammed Al Khusaibi</strong>, who works in logistics operations and procurement in Oman's energy sector and deals daily with customs clearance, logistics service providers and performance reporting. He holds a BSc in Operations Management from Sultan Qaboos University, is reading for an MSc in Logistics and Supply Chain Management at the University of London, and holds professional certifications from CILT and IPSCMI.</p>
    <p>Everything published here is personal and independent opinion and does not represent any employer.</p>
    <h2>How we treat numbers</h2>
    <p>The freight rates we publish are indicative spot-market figures, collected from forwarders and importers and shown as a median, and we say plainly when data is illustrative and when it is real. We do not sell quotes and take no commission from any forwarder.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Corrections, suggestions, and quotes that differ from what we publish: {{ site.contactEmail }} — or on <a href="{{ site.linkedin }}" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.</p>
  </div>
</article>
